 but i feel like  you're also  talking about  everybody  in in this film you're talking about a  lot of us aren't you hopefully you see  yourself in there i definitely do um but  yeah i feel like  the stuff that alice wants  is stuff that a lot of us want we want  to be seen and heard and some of us want  to be televised and  yeah  did you see an end to that  or is it just going to get worse i mean  it is infuriating to kind of walk  especially when i'm in new york just to  walk around and every you know  everyone's like this or like this and i  swear i have my questions on here which  is why i have my phone with me but i  mean it's it's literally gotten insane  now i don't see an end i think  everybody's going to have their own tv  network not just show  when technology improves and storage  improves i think everybody's going to  have it all i'm waiting for the moment  where someone actually does win the  lotto and then buys themselves a tv  network or a talk show it could really  happen it could really happen  tell me about  bringing kristen wiig on board was she  someone that you had in mind at any  point of writing this or developing it  was she a dream  candidate for you she was always in my  head i kind of didn't dare to dream  though because it seemed like such a  long shot but it happened i mean she was  definitely her combination of pathos and  sort of offbeat humor just seemed  perfect for alice and then when it  happened that was just amazing and then  everybody jumped on  because everybody wants to do a movie  with kristen wick because she's so  beloved so what was the process like of  finding out getting into her finding out  that she was interested did you then  meet with her or did she say yes just on  the printed page  i think she was very interested after  reading the script we did talk about  tone and stuff like that and um we got  it to her manager and waited a good  while for her to read it  and then she uh she said yes  was there a role i mean we were talking  out there in the lobby about just  between jennifer jason lee and james  marsden and wes bentley tim robbins john  q sac loretta devine i mean it's insane  all the people that signed on was there  one part that was  particularly hard to to nail to to get  someone great find that right person no  again it was like kristen wiig in the  script and everybody jumped on which is  just so like thrilling it was throwing i  was on set for most of the shoot and  watching these people that i've admired  like john cusack and jennifer jason lee  it was just absolutely thrilling to  watch them work how would you say your  background in improvisation and in  comedy in general how did that affect  your writing process your writing style  and  what you were able to contribute when  you were on the set i think it made me  very loose and collaborative a lot of  like whenever kristin reads an a  prepared statement which she does quite  a lot  um i usually would just like write those  right before she did them like or have a  few so i was able to kind of roll with  stuff improv is really good for that  improv is really good for writing  for a movie like this where your main  character  does have so many issues and is so out  there in a way  you really do need  characters who are surrogates for the  audience i would imagine my guess is  that the linda cardellini character fits  that bill the most did you have a couple  characters in mind that  were audience representatives in a way  sort of or just people who would sort of  ground the madness but definitely gina  linda cardellini's character was someone  who i think we can relate to in terms if  we've ever had anybody with mental  illness in our lives and so many of us  have we can relate to that sort of  exhaustion  and concern  and i think she brings so much to that  role she's so good  you were saying that this  whole project came out of a a dark place  in in your life i don't know how you  want to go  into that but ye